# Part II — Feature Pipeline Architecture

---

# 9. Feature Pipeline Overview

The Feature Pipeline transforms validated market data into production-ready model inputs through a deterministic sequence of stages.

No stage may be skipped, reordered, or modified without formal review.

The pipeline is defined as:

```
Validated Market Data
        │
        ▼
Input Verification
        │
        ▼
Time Alignment
        │
        ▼
Window Construction
        │
        ▼
Feature Computation
        │
        ▼
Feature Validation
        │
        ▼
Missing Value Handling
        │
        ▼
Metadata Attachment
        │
        ▼
Feature Registry Verification
        │
        ▼
Feature Store
        │
        ▼
Model Consumption
```

Each stage has clearly defined ownership and deterministic inputs and outputs.

---

# 10. Feature Registry

The Feature Registry is the authoritative catalog of every approved production feature.

The registry exists to prevent duplicate implementations, inconsistent naming, undocumented calculations, and uncontrolled feature growth.

Each production feature shall appear exactly once within the registry.

The registry defines:

* feature identifier
* canonical name
* mathematical definition
* owner
* implementation version
* required input datasets
* required lookback window
* output type
* valid value range
* production status
* approval history

Only registered features may be consumed by the production model.

Research features remain isolated from the production registry until formally promoted.

---

# 11. Feature Metadata

Every generated feature shall include sufficient metadata to guarantee traceability.

At minimum, metadata shall record:

* Feature ID
* feature name
* feature version
* generation timestamp
* dataset version
* source data version
* configuration version
* dependency versions
* window definition
* validation status
* integrity checksum

Metadata is considered part of the feature artifact and shall remain immutable after generation.

---

# 12. Feature Versioning

Feature definitions are immutable once released.

Any modification affecting numerical output constitutes a new feature version.

Version changes include, but are not limited to:

* formula revisions
* parameter changes
* normalization changes
* window modifications
* dependency updates
* preprocessing changes

Minor documentation updates do not require a new feature version unless they alter computational behavior.

Historic experiments shall always reference the exact feature version originally used.

---

# 13. Feature Dependencies

Every production feature shall explicitly declare its dependencies.

Dependencies may include:

* raw market datasets
* validated intermediate features
* symbol metadata
* approved configuration parameters

Implicit dependencies are prohibited.

Dependency graphs shall remain acyclic to ensure deterministic computation and simplify auditability.

A feature shall not depend on another feature whose production status is experimental or deprecated.

---

# 14. Feature Validation

Feature validation is mandatory before any feature becomes available to downstream consumers.

Validation shall confirm:

* input integrity
* numerical stability
* expected output type
* acceptable value range
* absence of undefined values unless explicitly permitted
* deterministic repeatability
* dependency consistency

Validation failures shall prevent publication to the Feature Store.

No downstream component shall override validation failures.
