# Part II

---

# 15. Leakage Prevention

Feature leakage is one of the highest-risk failure modes within quantitative research. The Feature Engine shall enforce strict safeguards to ensure that no feature contains information unavailable at the prediction timestamp.

Production models shall only consume information that would have been observable at the exact decision time.

Future market observations shall never influence current feature values.

The Feature Engine shall reject any computation that violates temporal ordering.

Examples of prohibited leakage include:

* future prices
* future returns
* future volume
* future volatility
* future order book state
* labels incorporated into features
* forward-filled information originating after the prediction timestamp

Researchers shall assume that any undocumented temporal dependency constitutes potential leakage until proven otherwise.

---

## REQ-FEAT-011

Production features shall never depend on future observations.

---

## REQ-FEAT-012

Every feature shall be computed using only information available at or before the prediction timestamp.

---

## REQ-FEAT-013

The Feature Engine shall validate temporal ordering before publishing feature outputs.

---

## REQ-FEAT-014

Feature definitions shall explicitly specify their observation timestamp.

---

# 16. Window Management

Many production features require historical observations over rolling windows.

Window definitions shall be explicit, deterministic, and immutable.

Every rolling feature shall define:

* window length
* observation frequency
* minimum observations
* boundary behavior
* warm-up requirements

Window sizes shall remain stable across research and production.

Changing a window definition constitutes a new feature version.

Incomplete windows shall not silently produce production feature values unless explicitly approved.

---

## Window Types

Version 1 supports only fixed historical windows.

Supported examples include:

* previous N bars
* previous N minutes
* previous N hours

Adaptive or dynamically expanding windows are outside Version 1.

---

## Warm-Up Period

Features requiring historical context shall define a mandatory warm-up period.

During warm-up:

* features may remain unavailable
* models shall not generate predictions
* missing windows shall not be approximated

Warm-up completion shall be deterministic for every symbol.

---

## REQ-FEAT-015

Every rolling feature shall declare its required lookback window.

---

## REQ-FEAT-016

Warm-up periods shall be explicitly defined.

---

## REQ-FEAT-017

Incomplete rolling windows shall not be substituted with estimated values.

---

# 17. Time Alignment

All production features shall reference a common prediction timestamp.

Features generated from multiple datasets shall be aligned before computation.

The Feature Engine shall maintain strict temporal consistency across:

* market prices
* volume
* derived statistics
* metadata
* model inputs

Timestamp normalization shall occur before feature computation.

Timezone conversions shall never occur inside feature calculations.

Time alignment rules shall remain identical across:

* research
* backtesting
* paper trading
* production

---

## Alignment Principles

Every feature row represents a single prediction instant.

All values within that row shall correspond to information known at that instant.

No feature shall be generated using partially completed market intervals.

---

## REQ-FEAT-018

Every feature vector shall correspond to one unique prediction timestamp.

---

## REQ-FEAT-019

All input datasets shall be temporally aligned before feature computation.

---

## REQ-FEAT-020

Timestamp normalization shall occur before any feature calculations.

---

# 18. Missing Value Policy

Missing values shall be treated as data quality events rather than routine conditions.

The Feature Engine shall never silently replace missing values.

Each feature shall define its missing value policy.

Permitted policies include:

* reject computation
* delay computation until sufficient data exists
* emit explicitly documented missing values
* use mathematically justified defaults approved during review

Implicit interpolation is prohibited unless formally documented.

Forward filling shall not be used when it introduces temporal distortion.

Production systems shall record every missing-value event.

---

## REQ-FEAT-021

Every production feature shall define a missing value policy.

---

## REQ-FEAT-022

Silent replacement of missing values is prohibited.

---

## REQ-FEAT-023

Missing-value events shall be logged for audit purposes.

